{
  "answer_markdown": "Common use cases for fine-tuning a model instead of relying on prompting include:\n\n1. **Customizing the model to generate responses in a specific format and tone**\n   - Fine-tuning can ensure the model outputs reliable, well-sourced, and professionally formatted responses, such as in legal applications [5].\n\n2. **Specializing the model for a specific topic or domain**\n   - Fine-tuning improves performance on domain-specific tasks, like legal translation or medical queries, by leveraging datasets tailored to those domains [1][5].\n\n3. **Improving performance through distillation from a stronger model**\n   - A smaller model can be fine-tuned to mimic the behavior of a larger model, reducing costs, latency, and token usage while maintaining efficiency [1][5].\n\n4. **Enhancing the model’s alignment with complex prompts**\n   - Fine-tuning can replicate the behavior of a model using a complex prompt without requiring the prompt, saving tokens and costs [1].\n\n5. **Reducing cost and latency**\n   - A small yet efficient fine-tuned model can perform tasks faster and cheaper than a larger model [1].\n\n6. **Teaching the model new facts or workflows**\n   - Fine-tuning allows the model to learn advanced tools or complicated workflows, improving its practical utility [1].",
  "citations": [
    {
      "n": 1,
      "quote": "Finetuning has a wide range of use cases, some of which include: Customizing the model to generate responses in a specific format and tone"
    },
